# Copy to `.env.local` (and/or `.env`). Next.js reads `.env.local`; `npm run db:migrate`
# loads `.env` then `.env.local` (local wins). Raw `npx prisma migrate deploy` only
# auto-loads `.env` in the project root—not `.env.local`—so prefer the npm script.
#
# --- PostgreSQL (Prisma) ---
# Local / any host: replace the username and password with a real PostgreSQL role.
# Do NOT leave the literal words USER or PASSWORD from older examples—Postgres will
# try to connect as a user named "USER" and you will see P1010 access denied.
# On many Mac setups the superuser matches your login: run `whoami` and use that name.
# Example (peer/trust socket user with empty password is rare in URLs—use a password you set):
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://your_postgres_username:your_postgres_password@localhost:5432/kdinsight?schema=public"
#
# Vercel: first-party "Vercel Postgres" was retired; new databases come from the
# Marketplace (e.g. Neon, Prisma Postgres). After you connect Storage → your
# project, the integration usually injects DATABASE_URL for all environments.
# If you only see POSTGRES_URL or POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL, duplicate that value into
# DATABASE_URL in Project Settings → Environment Variables (Prisma CLI/migrations
# read DATABASE_URL from prisma/schema.prisma).
#
# Optional Neon: they also provide DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED for migrations against a
# pooled DATABASE_URL — add directUrl to prisma/schema.prisma if you need that.

# --- Admin API auth ---
# Sign httpOnly cookies (HS256). Use a long random string, 32+ characters.
# Set this in Vercel for Production (+ Preview if you use the dashboard there).
ADMIN_JWT_SECRET="change-me-use-openssl-rand-hex-32-or-similar"

# --- Optional: seed a sign-in user (local / staging) ---
# After db:migrate, run: npm run db:seed
# Creates or updates this user so you can open /sign-in?flow=login
# SEED_USER_EMAIL="dev@kdinsight.local"
# SEED_USER_PASSWORD="use-a-long-random-password"
# SEED_USER_ROLE="ADMIN"
